{#- Shared blocks for the Data Commons migration notices. Inline styles only: mail clients
    strip <style>, so every rule has to travel with its element. -#}

{%- macro p() -%}<p style="margin:0 0 14px">{% endmacro %}

{%- macro counts(collections) -%}
<table cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0" border="0" style="margin:0 0 16px;font-size:14px">
{%- for c in collections %}
  <tr>
    <td style="padding:3px 16px 3px 0;font-family:monospace">{{ c.collection }}</td>
    <td style="padding:3px 16px 3px 0">{{ c.title }}</td>
    <td style="padding:3px 0;text-align:right;white-space:nowrap">
      {{ c.n_datasets }} dataset{{ '' if c.n_datasets == 1 else 's' }}</td>
  </tr>
{%- endfor %}
</table>
{%- endmacro %}

{%- macro unchanged() -%}
<p style="margin:0 0 14px"><b>What would not change.</b> No file is copied or moved. No path
changes. No permission changes. Your existing download links and the legacy Data Commons browse
URL keep working. The catalogue page links to the same anonymous WebDAV URLs that serve the data
now.</p>
{%- endmacro %}

{%- macro mapping(c) -%}
<p style="margin:0 0 8px"><b>What you would get.</b> An organization page at
<a href="https://dc.cyverse.org/organization/{{ c.slug }}">dc.cyverse.org/organization/{{ c.slug }}</a>,
and one dataset page per folder directly under <code>{{ c.path }}</code>
{%- if c.n_datasets %}, {{ c.n_datasets }} of them{% endif %}. The mapping is fixed: the collection
becomes the organization, each folder directly under it becomes a dataset, and the files and folders
inside a dataset become its resources. Anything deeper stays browsable over WebDAV without its own
catalogue entry, and a loose file sitting directly at the top of the collection is not published.</p>
{%- endmacro %}

{%- macro arrives(c) -%}
{%- if c.n_datasets == 0 -%}
<p style="margin:0 0 14px"><b>Worth knowing before you answer.</b> {{ c.note }} So accepting would
give you a described organization page and nothing under it until that changes. It is a Data Store
change on your side, not a catalogue one.</p>
{%- elif c.n_untitled -%}
<p style="margin:0 0 14px"><b>Being straight about what arrives.</b> The organization page is seeded
from your collection-level metadata, which is complete. The dataset pages are not.
{% if c.n_titled %}Only {{ c.n_titled }} of your {{ c.n_datasets }} folders carries a title or
description of its own{% else %}None of your {{ c.n_datasets }} folders carries a title or
description of its own{% endif %}, so {{ c.n_untitled }} of the dataset pages would arrive named
after the directory with an empty description. Writing those is real work and only your team can do it. That is
the main thing to weigh.</p>
{%- endif -%}
{%- endmacro %}

{%- macro admins() -%}
<li style="margin:0 0 8px">One or two people to administer the organization, with their email
addresses. Each has to sign in once at <a href="https://dc.cyverse.org">dc.cyverse.org</a> through
CyVerse SSO before I can add them: the catalogue has no local passwords, and no account exists until
first login.</li>
{%- endmacro %}

{%- macro licence(c) -%}
{%- if c.licence_action == 'decide' -%}
<li style="margin:0 0 8px">A licence we can record in machine-readable form, for example
CC-BY-4.0, CC0-1.0 or ODC-PDDL-1.0. Your rights statement is currently free text
&mdash; &ldquo;{{ c.licence_current|truncate(180) }}&rdquo; &mdash; which the catalogue cannot map, so
the page would read <i>License not specified</i>. The statement itself stays on the page either way.
Tell me the licence and I will write it into the Data Store metadata rather than typing it into the
catalogue, so the value survives.</li>
{%- elif c.licence_action == 'confirm' -%}
<li style="margin:0 0 8px">Confirmation on the licence. Your rights read
&ldquo;{{ c.licence_current|truncate(140) }}&rdquo;, which we would record as
<code>{{ c.licence_proposed }}</code>. Correct me if that is wrong.</li>
{%- endif -%}
{%- endmacro %}

{%- macro handover(c) -%}
{%- if c.collection == 'NCEMS' -%}
<p style="margin:0 0 14px"><b>After the load.</b> NCEMS is the exception among these: it already has
an organization and stays on the hourly metadata sync, so you keep editing in the Data Store and the
catalogue follows.</p>
{%- else -%}
<p style="margin:0 0 14px"><b>After the load.</b> The migration copies your Data Store metadata once.
From then on the catalogue record belongs to your admins and is edited in CKAN; changes to the iRODS
metadata no longer flow into it. Rolling back is cheap if you change your mind, because nothing was
ever moved: the catalogue entries are deleted and your data is untouched.</p>
{%- endif -%}
{%- endmacro %}

{%- macro signature(sender_name, sender_title) -%}
<p style="margin:0">{{ sender_name }}<br/>
{{ sender_title }}<br/>
University of Arizona</p>
{%- endmacro %}
